messages:
mia: proposing a new 'termination-gradient' rollout for our mixed spot/on-demand GPU pools. idea is to make scale-in and spot eviction handling gradual so we can drain long-running jobs and avoid sudden capacity cliffs. quick sketch below.

akshay: love the name. can you paste the tf diffs? also how does this change our instance selection logic?

mia: sure — high level: 1) add an autoscaling lifecycle hook + SSM document that sets a 'drain_score' tag on instances when scale-in is selected, 2) use a controller (lambda or k8s node controller) to gradually cordon/drain nodes based on drain_score (0.0..1.0), 3) adjust mixed_instances_policy overrides to include instance weighting so the ASG prefers lower-cost types but will fall back, 4) small tf rollout with canary ASG.

lina: P0 concern: kube drains need time. we should surface a per-instance drain window (env var) so batch jobs can checkpoint. also need to respect PodDisruptionBudgets.

mia: agreed. default drain window = 10m but configurable per pool. we'll add a metric to track how many drains exceed the window.

mia: terraform snippet (conceptual):
```hcl
resource "aws_autoscaling_group" "gpu_pool" {
  mixed_instances_policy {
    launch_template {
      launch_template_specification { launch_template_id = aws_launch_template.gpu.id, version = "$Latest" }
      override { instance_type = "g5.2xlarge", weighted_capacity = "2" }
      override { instance_type = "g5.xlarge", weighted_capacity = "1" }
    }
    instances_distribution {
      on_demand_base_capacity = 0
      on_demand_percentage_above_base_capacity = 10
      spot_allocation_strategy = "capacity-optimized"
    }
  }

  tag { key = "drain_score" value = "0.0" propagate_at_launch = true }
}

resource "aws_autoscaling_lifecycle_hook" "drain_hook" {
  name = "gpu-drain-hook"
  autoscaling_group_name = aws_autoscaling_group.gpu_pool.name
  default_result = "CONTINUE"
  heartbeat_timeout = 900
  lifecycle_transition = "autoscaling:EC2_INSTANCE_TERMINATING"
  notification_target_arn = aws_sqs_queue.drain_queue.arn
}
```

akshay: how do we ensure the ASG doesn't kill the instance immediately after tagging? the LF hook will buy time but we need the controller to respond to the hook quickly.

mia: plan: the lifecycle hook sends a message -> lambda picks it up -> lambda calls SSM RunCommand on the instance to run the drain script which: sets tag drain_score=1.0 -> calls kubeadm/cordon -> waits for pods to evict within drain window -> then completes lifecycle action. For preemptible spot interruptions (EC2 interruption notice), same handler path is triggered.

lina: can we avoid Lambda blowups if SSM fails? fallback: fallback agent installed in AMI that polls the instance metadata interruption endpoint and kicks off local drain on notice. terraform will ensure SSM agent + our drain agent baked into AMI.

sam: what's the expected cost delta? if we increase on-demand percentage to 10% for tail risk, are we losing savings?

mia: estimate: overall token-hour cost ~ 12-18% savings vs current pure on-demand depending on workload. increasing on-demand above base keeps p99 latency predictable; mixed policy + capacity-optimized spot should keep net savings ~15%. gradient reduces wasted work from abrupt evicts so effective savings are higher.

akshay: rollout plan? I'd prefer feature flag + canary pool in us-west-2 then expand. we should also run a short chaos job that forces spot interruption to validate graceful drain.

mia: rollout steps: 1) tf module change in a feature branch, 2) deploy canary ASG (1 AZ, small desired_capacity), 3) run bench jobs and simulated interruptions for 48h, 4) monitor drain overshoot and job restarts, 5) gradually increase desired_capacity and AZs, 6) flip module default on.

lina: add dashboard panels: drain_time_histogram, pods_evicted_per_drain, %drains_exceed_window, ASG_terminations_per_min, spot_fallback_count. also add alert if >5% drains exceed window in 1h.

sam: please post the tf plan here when you have it. i'll run cost sims against last 30d workloads.

deploy-bot: TF plan: changes to aws_autoscaling_group.gpu_pool, aws_autoscaling_lifecycle_hook.drain_hook, aws_launch_template.gpu (tags/ssm). plan approved for canary env: infra/canary/gpu_pool.

mia: thanks — i'll open a PR with the module and add the canary tfvars. keeping notes: - AMI must include drain-agent v0.3+, SSM enabled - default drain_window = 600s - canary size = 2 instances - enable debug logging for first 24h

akshay: one last ask — add a small script to convert drain_score -> prioritization order in our node-terminator controller so we terminate high drain_score last.

mia: yep will do. ok moving this to PR and tagging infra reviewers. :shipit:
